      "frozen_source_lines": [
        {
          "line": 199,
          "text": "5.1 Let $Z$ be a finite-dimensional Cartan-semisimple $\\mathfrak g$-module. Then $N\\otimes Z$ is again a genuine parabolic object: its weights are sums of weights of $N$ and $Z$, and local finiteness for $\\mathfrak p$ follows from $U(\\mathfrak p)n\\otimes Z$ being finite-dimensional for each $n$. It is finitely generated over $U(\\mathfrak m)$ by tensors of generators of $N$ with a basis of $Z$: induction on word length uses $xn\\otimes z=x(n\\otimes z)-n\\otimes xz$. Thus its $V$ image is defined on the stated domain. Apply the original-head right Skryabin counit $H_N\\otimes_W Q_{\\chi,\\mathrm{right}}\\to D_\\chi N$, $f\\otimes\\overline a\\mapsto fa$. Tensor it with $Z^\\vee$, apply right Wh and use step 2.3 to obtain $H_N\\circledast_{\\mathrm{right}}Z^\\vee\\cong H_{N\\otimes Z}$. Combine this actual counit with the module pairing of step 4.1 and take finite duals. This gives the natural $W$ isomorphism $\\nu_{N,Z}:V(N\\otimes Z)\\to V(N)\\circledast Z$ for EVERY genuine parabolic object $N$, including objects without an induced-module realization. Coassociativity, counit associativity and the inverse product formulas of step 3.2 make its two iterated tensor constructions agree under transported associativity. Naturality in a map of finite tensor factors follows from its ordinary dual map and the basis-independent evaluation pairing; hence adjacent flips are intertwined. [F2, F6, step 2.3, step 4.1, construct, algebra]"
        },
        {
          "line": 200,
          "text": ""
        },
        {
          "line": 201,
          "text": "6.1 Compute the actual Casimir coefficients in ordinary box matrix units. On the LEFT lift, the coefficient at $v_k$ after $\\Omega=\\sum E_{ab}\\otimes E_{ba}$ is $\\sum_hE_{h,k}\\eta_{\\mathrm{tail}}X_{hj}$. Positive matrices give $(E_{h,k}-\\delta_{hk}\\rho_k^{\\mathrm{tail}})X_{hj}$ after undoing the shift. Negative matrices give $\\chi(E_{h,k})X_{hj}-X_{kj}$ by step 1.9. Their total scalar is $-\\rho_k^{\\mathrm{tail}}-\\sum_{b>\\operatorname{col}(k)}q_b=q_{\\operatorname{col}(k)}-n$. On the RIGHT lift the coefficient at $v^h$ is $\\sum_k\\eta_{\\mathrm{head}}Y_{ik}E_{h,k}$; step 2.2 moves negative matrices left, giving $\\chi(E_{h,k})Y_{ik}-Y_{ih}$, and the total scalar is $-\\rho_h^{\\mathrm{head}}-\\sum_{b<\\operatorname{col}(h)}q_b=q_{\\operatorname{col}(h)}-n$. Multiplying by $Y$ on the left or $X$ on the right therefore gives the SAME entries $C_{j,i}=\\sum_{\\operatorname{col}(h)\\le\\operatorname{col}(k)}Y_{i,k}E_{h,k}X_{h,j}+\\sum_k(q_{\\operatorname{col}(k)}-n)Y_{i,k}X_{k,j}+\\sum_{\\operatorname{row}(h)=\\operatorname{row}(k),\\,\\operatorname{col}(h)=\\operatorname{col}(k)-1}Y_{i,h}X_{k,j}$. There is no extra displacement sign on the ordinary $E_{h,k}$; signs are already in $X,Y$. The entries lie in $W$ because the Casimir preserves the actual Whittaker subspace. The module pairing now intertwines the two transported Casimirs, while the continuous tensor pairing transposes the source Casimir term by term. Hence $\\nu$ intertwines the source and target Casimir maps. With flip compatibility this also intertwines their polynomial and generalized-eigenspace residue projections, with the usual contragredient retained for reverse translation. [F2, F3, F6, step 1.9, step 2.2, step 4.1, step 5.1, construct, algebra]"
